    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and the full test suite is green. Here's a summary of what I changed.\n\n## What the change does\n\nWhen a `POST /api/owners` request sets `sharesHousehold: true` and there's already an owner with the same last name and address, the new owner is allowed through (existing behavior) **and** now both the joiner and the existing household member(s) are stamped with the same stable `householdId`, which is returned on the owner resource.\n\n## Changes\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added a read-only `householdId` field to the `Owner` response schema, so it flows into the generated `OwnerDto` and is returned by `GET /api/owners/{id}` (via MapStruct's name-based mapping).\n\n2. **`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 Added the `householdId` field (`@Column(name = \"household_id\")`) with getter/setter.\n\n3. **DB schemas** (`db/{hsqldb,h2,mysql,postgres}/schema.sql`) \u2014 Added the `household_id` column to the `owners` table. The tests run on hsqldb with `ddl-auto=none`, so the schema.sql change is what actually creates the column; the others keep the dialects in sync.\n\n4. **`rest/function/owner/AssignHousehold.java`** (new) \u2014 A pipeline step that, only when `sharesHousehold=true`, finds existing owners sharing the normalized last name + address, chooses a household id (reusing one already present, otherwise deriving a deterministic `H-<sha256 prefix>` from the normalized key), stamps it on the new owner, and back-fills + saves it onto existing members. The deterministic derivation guarantees every joiner computes the same id regardless of ordering or transaction visibility \u2014 which is what makes the identifier \"stable\" and \"shared\".\n\n5. **`officefloor/rest/api/owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 Inserted the `household-assign` step (under `transaction` governance) between `build` and `customerCode`, so it runs on the built entity before it's saved.\n\nThe normalization (trim, collapse internal whitespace, lowercase) matches the existing `CheckUniqueHousehold` collision logic exactly, so the household that gets a shared id is precisely the one that opting in was allowed to join.\n\nTest result: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 `BUILD SUCCESS`.",
